{
  "dom_id": "step-q51405",
  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069816/assets/1e11260301be8c0bb19a9fd47ffbbf71cc5fa4d94dbeae57af934b76fb17f0e1.png)\n\nA particle has a speed of $v(t)$ meters per second after $t$ seconds. The graph of $v(t)$ is shown above. Approximate the rate of change of speed when $t=3$ using a central difference approximation with step size $h=1.$",
  "worked_solution": "We use the central difference approximation\n\n$$\n{v}^{\u2032}(x)\u2248\\frac{v(t+h)-g(t-h)}{2h},\n$$\n\n \nat the time $t=2$ and step size $h=1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}^{\u2032}(2) & \u2248\\frac{v(3+1)-v(3-1)}{2(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{v(4)-v(2)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3-1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{2} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change of speed at the instant of time $t=2$ is approximately $1\\,\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2}.$",
